\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:2.3}
Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces, let $f\colon X\to Y$ be a function, and let $E\subseteq X$ be equipped with the induced metric $d_X|_{E\times E}$. Fix $x_0\in E$. If $f$ is continuous at $x_0$ (as a map on $X$), then the restriction $f|_E\colon E\to Y$ is continuous at $x_0$ (as a map on $E$). In particular, if $f$ is continuous on $X$, then $f|_E$ is continuous on $E$.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:2.3}
Assume that $f$ is continuous at $x_0\in E$. Let $\varepsilon>0$ be given. By continuity of $f$ at $x_0$ in $X$, there exists $\delta>0$ such that for all $x\in X$, if $d_X(x,x_0)<\delta$, then $d_Y(f(x),f(x_0))<\varepsilon$.

Now let $x\in E$ satisfy $d_X|_{E\times E}(x,x_0)<\delta$. Since $d_X|_{E\times E}(x,x_0)=d_X(x,x_0)$, it follows that $d_Y(f|_E(x),f|_E(x_0))=d_Y(f(x),f(x_0))<\varepsilon$. This is exactly the $\varepsilon$--$\delta$ condition for continuity of $f|_E$ at $x_0$.

For the final statement, if $f$ is continuous on $X$, then $f$ is continuous at every $x_0\in E$, hence $f|_E$ is continuous at every $x_0\in E$ by the first part.
\end{proof}

