\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:2.9}
Let $X$ be a topological space and let $m,n\ge 0$ be integers. Suppose that $f:X\to\mathbb{R}^m$ and $g:X\to\mathbb{R}^n$ are continuous (with the standard Euclidean topologies). Define $(f,g):X\to\mathbb{R}^{m+n}$ by
\[
\label{eq:2.u38}
(f,g)(x) := (f(x),g(x))\in \mathbb{R}^m\times \mathbb{R}^n \cong \mathbb{R}^{m+n}.
\]
Then $(f,g)$ is continuous.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:2.9}
Let $\pi_1:\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m$ and $\pi_2:\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ be the coordinate projections. The product topology on $\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n$ has a basis consisting of sets of the form $U\times V$ with $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m$ open and $V\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ open.

Fix such a basic open set $U\times V$. Then
\[
\label{eq:2.u39}
(f,g)^{-1}(U\times V)=\{x\in X: f(x)\in U\text{ and } g(x)\in V\}=f^{-1}(U)\cap g^{-1}(V).
\]
Since $f$ and $g$ are continuous, $f^{-1}(U)$ and $g^{-1}(V)$ are open in $X$, hence their intersection is open. Therefore the preimage of every basic open set in $\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n$ is open in $X$, so $(f,g):X\to\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n$ is continuous.

Under the standard identification $\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}^n\cong\mathbb{R}^{m+n}$, the topologies agree, so $(f,g):X\to\mathbb{R}^{m+n}$ is continuous.
\end{proof}

