\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:2.13}
Let \(f: \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}\) be a continuous function. For each \(x \in \mathbb{R}\), the function \(y \mapsto f(x, y)\) is continuous on \(\mathbb{R}\), and for each \(y \in \mathbb{R}\), the function \(x \mapsto f(x, y)\) is continuous on \(\mathbb{R}\). Thus a function \(f(x, y)\) which is jointly continuous in \((x, y)\) is also continuous in each variable \(x, y\) separately.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:2.13}
Fix $x_0\in\mathbb{R}$ and set $g=g_{x_0}$. To prove that $g$ is continuous at an arbitrary $y_0\in\mathbb{R}$, let $\varepsilon>0$ be given. Since $f$ is continuous at $(x_0,y_0)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, there exists $\delta>0$ such that for all $(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^2$,
\[
\label{eq:2.u55}
\sqrt{(x-x_0)^2+(y-y_0)^2}<\delta \quad\Rightarrow\quad |f(x,y)-f(x_0,y_0)|<\varepsilon.
\]
If $|y-y_0|<\delta$, then
\[
\label{eq:2.u56}
\sqrt{(x_0-x_0)^2+(y-y_0)^2}=|y-y_0|<\delta,
\]
so $|g(y)-g(y_0)|=|f(x_0,y)-f(x_0,y_0)|<\varepsilon$. This proves that $g$ is continuous at $y_0$, and since $y_0$ was arbitrary, $g$ is continuous on $\mathbb{R}$.

The proof for $h_{y_0}(x)=f(x,y_0)$ is analogous: continuity of $f$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ implies that for $|x-x_0|<\delta$ one has $\sqrt{(x-x_0)^2+(y_0-y_0)^2}=|x-x_0|<\delta$, hence $|h_{y_0}(x)-h_{y_0}(x_0)|<\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

