\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:2.15}
[Maximum principle]
Let $(X,d)$ be a compact metric space and let $f:X\to\mathbb{R}$ be continuous.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The function $f$ is bounded on $X$.
\item If $X\neq\emptyset$, then there exist points $x_{\max},x_{\min}\in X$ such that
\[
\label{eq:2.u61}
f(x_{\max})=\max_{x\in X} f(x),\qquad f(x_{\min})=\min_{x\in X} f(x).
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:2.15}
If $X=\emptyset$, then $f(X)=\emptyset$ is bounded and the second assertion is vacuous.

Assume $X\neq\emptyset$. Consider the image set $K:=f(X)\subset\mathbb{R}$. Since $f$ is continuous and $X$ is compact, $K$ is compact. In particular, $K$ is bounded, so there exists $M>0$ such that $|y|\le M$ for all $y\in K$. Hence $|f(x)|\le M$ for all $x\in X$, so $f$ is bounded.

Since $K\subset\mathbb{R}$ is nonempty and compact, it is closed and bounded, and therefore attains its supremum and infimum. Set $m:=\min K$ and $M:=\max K$. By definition of image, there exist $x_{\min},x_{\max}\in X$ such that $f(x_{\min})=m$ and $f(x_{\max})=M$. Then for all $x\in X$, one has $m\le f(x)\le M$, so $f(x_{\min})=\min_{x\in X} f(x)$ and $f(x_{\max})=\max_{x\in X} f(x)$.
\end{proof}

